<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>name</key><string>Wiki Link References (body)</string>

    <!-- Body wiki links need no syntax of ours: the built-in Markdown package
         already scopes them. BOTH BRACKETS FALL INSIDE THE META SCOPE, so both
         have to be subtracted. In its 'link-ref-wiki' context the opening '[['
         is the pushing match and the closing ']]' is the popping match, and a
         'meta_scope' covers the text of both, and that is what separates it from
         'meta_content_scope', which would have covered neither. The brackets
         additionally carry punctuation.definition.link.begin/end.markdown, and
         those are the only handles there are on them.

         Subtracting both is therefore what isolates the bare target name, and
         each omission fails the same way: with the end left in every symbol is
         indexed as "name]]", with the begin left in as "[[name", and a lookup
         by filename never hits either. THE SECOND FORM IS WHAT SHIPPED, for
         long enough to be worth recording. It hid because it was symmetrical
         with a matching mistake in WikiLinks.py, where name_under_caret() stripped
         a trailing ']]' and not a leading '[[' on the same false premise, so
         shift+f12 ON a body link queried "[[francis" and worked, while
         shift+f12 anywhere else in the note queried "francis" and returned the
         frontmatter hits alone. The whole body corpus was indexed and
         unreachable. Note also what did NOT catch it: the reference counts in
         CLAUDE.md were modelled in Python over the corpus rather than read back
         out of Sublime, and a model of the selector cannot disagree with the
         selector. What settled it was grepping Data/Index/*.ldb, where the
         stored names read literally '[[eligible4ODA', '[[MASE', '[[CDP'.

         THE OTHER TWO SUBTRACTIONS ARE NOT TIDINESS, THEY ARE THE PRICE OF NOT
         DESTROYING TWO OTHER SYMBOL LISTS. Read this before removing either.

         A .tmPreferences entry does not add to what other entries say. At each
         point the HIGHEST SCORING selector wins and ITS settings are the only
         ones that apply there. This entry matches two scopes and one of them
         is deep on the stack, so it outscores anything matching the container:

             markup.heading            MarkdownEditing, Symbol List - Heading
             entity.name.tag.todo      ours, Symbol List - ToDos

         Since this entry says showInSymbolList 0 and showInIndexedSymbolList 0,
         winning inside one of those means punching a hole in the middle of the
         container's own symbol region, which then survives only as the two
         fragments either side of the link. The reported symptom was a project
         symbol called "]]"; the real loss was the heading text itself, which
         stopped being findable under the words it is written with.

         MarkdownEditing uses this same mechanism deliberately in
         'Symbol List - Hide.tmPreferences', which punches meta.toc-list.id out
         of a heading. That is harmless only because an id sits at the END of a
         line, so one run survives. A wiki link sits in the middle.

         WHAT THE SUBTRACTIONS COST, measured on the vault rather than guessed:

             219 headings hold 477 links, of which 233 point at a note that
             exists. Of the 211 names reachable ONLY from a heading exactly two
             are real notes (giorgiaCaropreso, simonaRuggieri); the rest are
             dangling short forms like [[mpho]] and [[john??]] that no rename
             could ever have touched.

             304 of the 497 open todos hold 468 links, and these are the
             expensive ones: 464 of them point at a real note, and six notes
             are reachable only from a todo.

         So roughly 700 real backlink rows are not listed, and a rename leaves
         those links stale. That is a blind spot, so it is announced rather
         than hidden: WikiLinks.py puts HEADING_NOTE in the rename confirmation
         dialog and in Data/Logs/WikiLinks.log on every single run.

         THE EXPERIMENT THAT WOULD WIN THEM ALL BACK is shipped inert beside
         this file as

             Reference List - Wiki In Symbols.tmPreferences.disabled

         and takes about a minute to settle. It turns on an undocumented
         question: whether Sublime builds a symbol region from adjacent points
         that are merely all FLAGGED, or only from adjacent points owned by the
         same ENTRY. If the former, the containers stay whole and every link
         comes back and these two subtractions can go. If the latter, nothing
         is worse than it was when the bug was reported. -->
    <key>scope</key>
    <string>text.html.markdown meta.link.reference.wiki.description.markdown - punctuation.definition.link.begin.markdown - punctuation.definition.link.end.markdown - markup.heading - entity.name.tag.todo</string>

    <key>settings</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- References, not definitions, and deliberately so. A wiki link is a
             USE of a note, not a declaration of one, and SYMBOL_TYPE_REFERENCE
             is what window.symbol_locations() filters on. Staying out of the
             definition namespace also keeps these clear of the '_note' tokens
             and of MarkdownEditing's '#'-prefixed headings, which all share one
             flat global namespace (see CLAUDE.md).

             Out of the per-file list too: ctrl+R is for navigating the file you
             are in, and a note's own outbound links are not landmarks. -->
        <key>showInSymbolList</key><integer>0</integer>
        <key>showInIndexedSymbolList</key><integer>0</integer>
        <key>showInIndexedReferenceList</key><integer>1</integer>

        <!-- NO symbolIndexTransformation, on purpose. The selector above
             already yields exactly the target name, so the indexed symbol is
             literally the text in the file, and a rename can compare it against
             a filename with no decoding step in between. Symbol List
             ToDos.tmPreferences records what it cost, once, to make a symbol's
             identity hostage to a regex. -->

        <!-- Cosmetic only. WikiLinks.py labels a backlink row by kind when one
             is present and behaves identically when it is not: no shipped
             package uses this key, so whether it survives into
             SymbolLocation.kind on an indexed REFERENCE is unverified. Nothing
             depends on the answer. It differs from the frontmatter file's kind
             purely so the question can be settled by looking at a result. -->
        <key>kind</key><string>markup.list</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
